{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"eaf71e68d5a41b2e","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Talismans- Durations.md","x":1020,"y":677,"width":420,"height":383},
		{"id":"e2758f3b98cdf528","type":"text","text":"==How to play-Cain core==","x":30,"y":-55,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"d22a1f816f7de178","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Dice roll.md","x":-583,"y":-229,"width":523,"height":649},
		{"id":"6407b6220e59b20b","type":"text","text":"==Types of dice rolls==","x":-650,"y":-591,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"49158ee29e2f8bf2","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Risky.md","x":-300,"y":-1201,"width":560,"height":761},
		{"id":"118f0e59f2e43e7d","type":"text","text":"==Admin-wise==","x":-1020,"y":-599,"width":140,"height":50},
		{"id":"a21323693be50c2a","type":"text","text":"==Player-wise==","x":-583,"y":-751,"width":154,"height":50},
		{"id":"6d38aeb22cd2d442","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Fate rolls.md","x":-1600,"y":-701,"width":420,"height":391},
		{"id":"1b3f433e810747d4","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Admin rolls.md","x":-1600,"y":-255,"width":450,"height":515},
		{"id":"b95b39c33d08ce88","type":"text","text":"==Failure on dice roll-risky roll response==","x":-1160,"y":285,"width":313,"height":60},
		{"id":"02941a8ed61c06be","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Consequences.md","x":-2280,"y":420,"width":905,"height":1260},
		{"id":"a3ac85128c29d220","type":"text","text":"==Rolling a 6 in a risk die==","x":-1108,"y":510,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"bbaa10203f549486","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Pasted image 20261004113204.png","x":400,"y":-168,"width":400,"height":286},
		{"id":"fba6e77d03ff4f35","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Straight rolls.md","x":-686,"y":-1560,"width":386,"height":317},
		{"id":"1f361438db0ea2fc","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Hard.md","x":-1150,"y":-1602,"width":426,"height":401},
		{"id":"5d744cd55f142e33","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Impossible.md","x":-1590,"y":-1243,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"a8250981d5ad8f5f","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Upsides.md","x":-1180,"y":734,"width":460,"height":426},
		{"id":"4f607ce73b21951c","type":"text","text":"==How to adquire advantages-improving dice rolls==","x":-341,"y":590,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"5b37613216613170","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Teamwork.md","x":-491,"y":772,"width":511,"height":648},
		{"id":"cac3a14514aeef76","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Setup.md","x":-1120,"y":1277,"width":479,"height":806},
		{"id":"d1203c8783d6ffd8","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Improving a roll.md","x":120,"y":426,"width":533,"height":692},
		{"id":"de32cd4cb1940236","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Divine agony.md","x":140,"y":1277,"width":600,"height":723},
		{"id":"5b1a7b5f5640d261","type":"text","text":"==Talismans==","x":449,"y":289,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"8cb0b8633f06b0ce","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Talismans- what and how.md","x":999,"y":108,"width":441,"height":432},
		{"id":"476c1682c28b1fa1","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Talismans- Progress, Results and examples.md","x":1540,"y":220,"width":505,"height":692},
		{"id":"60ea06657cf8b9ca","type":"file","file":"Captura de pantalla 2026-10-16 130028.png","x":480,"y":-931,"width":400,"height":370},
		{"id":"96ea62ebe9a0a511","type":"text","text":"==What is category==","x":324,"y":-440,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"53c4b746313065fb","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Anything mundane an exorcist does to try and harm or subdue a supernatural force such as a sin is ==always hard, no matter what.== Their capabilities and equipment are the general capabilities of a (well trained) human, usually rated at CAT 0.\n","x":1039,"y":-831,"width":401,"height":231},
		{"id":"bc8a85343c9956ca","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Much like natural disasters, both sins and exorcists are rated by ==Category==, generally indicating their power, usually written as ==CAT==. As each increase in category, the ==speed==, ==scale==, and strength of their capabilities increase drastically.","x":940,"y":-440,"width":360,"height":240},
		{"id":"beb51f111d0bc095","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Admin- how to judge cat in actions.md","x":1520,"y":-636,"width":464,"height":632},
		{"id":"cce9ff58d1b657b0","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Your ==agenda== describes your character’s motivations, role in the group, and way they approach the world. It has a couple roles: \n> \n> • Your agenda allows you to ==gain experience==. At the end of a session, you will check if you followed the regular agenda items or any bolded agenda items, which are harder to fulfill, then gain experience based on the answer. \n> \n> • Your agenda gives you a suite of ==abilities==. You can gain more abilities from your agenda by spending experience \n> \n> • Your agenda may give you a few ways to regain resources","x":-1176,"y":-2228,"width":478,"height":440},
		{"id":"2ad94e419f757240","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • You can carry over abilities or ==bolded== agenda items from your old agenda to your new agenda.","x":-2136,"y":-2560,"width":354,"height":147},
		{"id":"87b2cae72a980044","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Between missions you can actually ==swap agendas== if you feel like a new agenda would fit your character. This could be due an experience within the mission, another character moment, or just because you feel like your current agenda isn’t fitting your character.","x":-1679,"y":-2480,"width":378,"height":252},
		{"id":"c4c228063231300d","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Your agenda ==can also be modified mid hunt==, depending on mission parameters. The main way for this to happen is through fighting sins, which may add agenda items. The Admin is also free to write new agenda elements and offer them as rewards or for completing certain story beats.","x":-2640,"y":-2354,"width":360,"height":280},
		{"id":"0759b5e7f72d6b5d","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • If you swap agendas again, you can keep carrying over abilities gained from any source, though you ==can’t have more than five agenda abilities== at any given time, and must choose which to keep and which to discard.","x":-2136,"y":-2334,"width":354,"height":240},
		{"id":"8e2d1e2c2ebbc627","type":"text","text":"==Skills list==","x":496,"y":-2662,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"de697e44957addec","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Skill-Authority.md","x":952,"y":-2832,"width":540,"height":510},
		{"id":"e27197f1fa44b1f0","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Skill-Surveillance.md","x":1577,"y":-2829,"width":515,"height":527},
		{"id":"bd2a2b3e2a1b1d8c","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Skill-Negotiation.md","x":2172,"y":-2829,"width":520,"height":527},
		{"id":"fcae690908049d3d","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Skill-Investigation.md","x":952,"y":-2242,"width":500,"height":568},
		{"id":"766a1785a1604345","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Skill-Force.md","x":2132,"y":-2202,"width":540,"height":528},
		{"id":"6824078d03819e9a","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Skill-Covert.md","x":952,"y":-1562,"width":500,"height":540},
		{"id":"6a22c66d19034740","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Skill-Coordination.md","x":1512,"y":-1512,"width":480,"height":440},
		{"id":"68ffdb340f7da6a3","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Skill-Connection.md","x":2092,"y":-1562,"width":500,"height":560},
		{"id":"71e2726f910df503","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Skill-Interfacing.md","x":1552,"y":-2202,"width":515,"height":528},
		{"id":"8fd519b9957aba16","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Skill-Psyche.md","x":2932,"y":-2442,"width":540,"height":480},
		{"id":"20f33582773c7aef","type":"file","file":"Cain-core book-rule summaries/Skill-Conditioning.md","x":2092,"y":-902,"width":460,"height":480},
		{"id":"7e305614015d1315","type":"text","text":"==Player skills==","x":-302,"y":-2262,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"3891079af1043e87","type":"text","text":"Blasphemy accessible by default","x":3720,"y":-2237,"width":280,"height":50},
		{"id":"95ad0d4968894f02","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==BLAST (1 burst cost)==\n> \n> Instant, Short \n> \n> Spend a psyche burst and roll PSYCHE to produce a weaponized form of concentrated psychic energy in melee or short range. The specific look and feel of this basic exorcist skill varies between exorcists. The strength of this blast scales with CAT.\n> \n> When your exorcist produces a blast, they might: \n> \n> • Imbue a slash of a blade or a shot with a ranged weapon with psychic energy \n> \n> • Shoot an invisible ball of force from their fingers \n> \n> • Fire scathing lightning \n> \n> • Shoot a bead of ghostly, frigid fire \n> \n> Unlike your mundane service weapons, blast is a supernatural weapon and therefore scales with CAT and doesn’t become hard by default when used against sins.","x":3520,"y":-2880,"width":680,"height":527},
		{"id":"f2894ba310c20a97","type":"text","text":"==Psyche burst==","x":-145,"y":-1590,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"2b6d1262927c4d27","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==Using any blasphemy power usually requires a psyche burst==. Some can spend multiple for extra benefit.","x":343,"y":-1589,"width":337,"height":149},
		{"id":"608914dbda3af213","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • You can always spend a ==psyche burst== to add +1 advantage die to any roll (yours or an ally’s) by using your blasphemies in a creative way. You can do this once per roll. \n> \n> • You always start a hunt with ==three psyche bursts== \n> \n> • You can recover them by resting during a hunt (see pg. 38) or through the uses of abilities.","x":105,"y":-2040,"width":455,"height":308},
		{"id":"490fae4def0e7c43","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> You can spend an ==advance== to gain a new blasphemy instead of gaining a new blasphemy power. You gain that blasphemy’s ==passive ability==, ==gain one power== from it and can gain additional powers in that blasphemy moving forward. However: \n> \n> • reduce your cap for sin overflow by 1 per extra blasphemy taken. You do this by crossing out a sin box on your sheet \n> \n> • Your xp cap for advances increases by +1 per extra blasphemy taken","x":-1051,"y":-255,"width":342,"height":417},
		{"id":"fc31c2707600a1e1","type":"text","text":"==Taking additional blasphemies== ","x":-950,"y":-380,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"3cba6e3e40c8b36f","type":"text","text":"==Sin==","x":-741,"y":-2484,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"5bef44e3aaf006c2","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> An exorcist is a limited being. ==The targets of their hunts are not==. An exorcist’s reserves of power are limited unless they take time to recover their strength - a luxury they may not have.","x":-754,"y":-2827,"width":314,"height":250},
		{"id":"20b8fccb9f9485d2","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==Hitting your sin cap== at any point during a mission will put you into ==sin overflow== - but only at the end of a scene. This means an exorcist can gain limitless sin and tap an incredible source of power if they are already over capacity.","x":-741,"y":-3168,"width":301,"height":268},
		{"id":"a8aaf5a374735e7c","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==Sin is not reset per hunt, but is instead halved, rounding up, after a mission is over (providing you survived).==","x":-1260,"y":-2763,"width":320,"height":186},
		{"id":"6bff0edcd4d4af4a","type":"text","text":"==Sin overflow==","x":-253,"y":-3066,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"f13ea67e42b22e9f","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> If an exorcist would meet or exceed their ==sin capacity==, they go into ==sin overflow== at the end of the scene. They lose control of the psychic energies in their body, which start to run wild and begin their transformation into a ==new sin==. A character in Sin Overflow has two choices: ==give up== or ==resist==.","x":140,"y":-3184,"width":340,"height":296},
		{"id":"6186582f3960090e","type":"text","text":"==Give up==","x":184,"y":-3384,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"44714817772a252a","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> A character that gives up effectively ceases to be a player character and may work with the Admin to determine the Sin they become. They become an antagonist and control is handed over to the Admin. \n> \n> They make one final choice, which is to ==flee== or ==fight==. Fighting immediately forces a conflict scene with an ==Imago== (see page 148). The new Sin may become a target of a later hunt, or a greater antagonist. \n> \n> If the group feels like it is a good time to break and reassess the situation, CAIN may also call the mission, ending it early. An emergence of a new Sin is a disaster that drastically changes the parameters of the mission.","x":101,"y":-3960,"width":419,"height":460},
		{"id":"fba5ee1435678865","type":"text","text":"==Resist==","x":696,"y":-3066,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"cac7bb8cdf4cde8a","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> A character that makes this choice must make a resistance check. \n> \n> • They must roll a d6, adding their number of sin marks (see below). If they have no sin marks, they ==automatically pass this test== \n> \n> • If a character has no sin boxes left, they fail this test \n> \n> • on a total result of 6 or less, they ==pass== and can keep control \n> \n> • on a total result of 7 or higher, they ==fail== and must ==give up== \n> \n> • a result of ‘1’ on the die always passes \n> \n> A character that keeps control ==clears all sin, permanently ticks off two sin boxes==, and gains a ==sin mark==.","x":1100,"y":-3354,"width":520,"height":438},
		{"id":"aa5d765d6dbbce88","type":"text","text":"==Sin mark==","x":1235,"y":-3640,"width":250,"height":57},
		{"id":"2494e877dc48ca55","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> A ==sin mark== is a physical manifestation of wild power overflow on your body, evidence of your partial transformation into a sin. You can find a list of sin marks, their effects, and their table, on pg. 86. A sin mark is a new, permanent part of your character. It grants new, beneficial power, and grants you new abilities.","x":1180,"y":-4024,"width":360,"height":294},
		{"id":"06673cdb949d19e7","type":"text","text":"==Player==","x":-493,"y":-1764,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"52582b6eca3033cf","type":"text","text":"==Agendas==","x":-849,"y":-1734,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"8b645e3a6a12273c","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> It is inevitable that exorcists put themselves in harm’s way during a mission - not only physical injury, but also exhaustion, fatigue, illness, or mental strain. These kinds of challenges usually would massively impair or incapacitate a regular human. \n> \n> Fortunately, ==you are an exorcist== and always take harm by taking stress. When you would take mental or physical harm, ==you slash your exorcists’s execution talisman with stress instead.==","x":-2280,"y":-1896,"width":380,"height":377},
		{"id":"a4e21d49d07671e3","type":"text","text":"==Stress==","x":-1615,"y":-1737,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"3dca48d4aad801d2","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==•1 slash==: Pain, strain, tiredness, suffering, minor injury \n> \n> ==• 2/3 slashes==: Crippling or even fatal to most humans, injury, exhaustion, bleeding, deprivation, poison, etc. \n> \n> ==• 4+ slashes==: Instantly and messily fatal to humans","x":-2792,"y":-1856,"width":305,"height":297},
		{"id":"a424e95c647949ac","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Your talisman starts with capacity for ==6 slashes==. You ==always start every hunt without any slashes on the talisman==, fresh and ready to go","x":-2246,"y":-1380,"width":313,"height":198},
		{"id":"cd69c075b0ae8324","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> If your talisman fills up, clear all slashes and take an ==injury==. Any remaining stress ‘rolls over’ and fills the talisman again. \n> \n> Some stress is ==nonlethal.== Nonlethal stress can ==never fill your talisman up== (it always leaves 1 slash open, and you ignore any excess). \n> \n> Taking stress represents avoiding or mitigating incoming harm. Taking injuries does not.","x":-3006,"y":-1340,"width":367,"height":340},
		{"id":"fa0e0f2e0f2bc6ce","type":"text","text":"==Injuries==","x":-2948,"y":-766,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"0a620e47b7f90070","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Injuries represent direct physical or mental harm to your being, such as: \n> \n> • a twisted ankle \n> \n> • a bleeding gunshot \n> \n> • poison taking hold of your body \n> \n> • deep mental distress \n> \n> • exhaustion","x":-2443,"y":-897,"width":354,"height":323},
		{"id":"aa431780b3032f85","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Exorcists can fight and act normally through taking an injury. Taking an injury clears all stress, but then ==reduces your maximum stress by 1== while you have it. This ==stacks for each injury you have==.","x":-2430,"y":-310,"width":328,"height":230},
		{"id":"584e4111a1077474","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Injuries have the following additional rules: \n> \n> • You heal all injuries after a mission is over, and always start missions with no injuries. \n> \n> • You also heal one injury if you ==defy your fate== instead of dying instantly (see below) \n> \n> • If you gain 3 injuries, remaining stress does not roll over and you are ==on the brink of death==. If you would mark ==any== more stress, you will suffer ==instant death==.","x":-2948,"y":-343,"width":348,"height":443},
		{"id":"e6378469591e467e","type":"text","text":"==Instant death==","x":-3610,"y":-152,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"a2b0d2d3544dc169","type":"text","text":"==Defy your fate==","x":-3477,"y":275,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"d26c36ffd11ead48","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> An exorcist that marks any stress when on the ==brink of death== is ==killed instantly== and ==can describe in what manner to the Admin==. \n> \n> • Other abilities (from sins, etc) may also inflict instant death \n> \n> • If an exorcist would take 7 or more slashes on stress from a single event, after reductions, they also suffer instant death \n> \n> • An exorcist that suffers instant death may ==defy their fate== and suffer ==sin overflow== instead of dying. Doing so heals them ==one injury==.","x":-4280,"y":-113,"width":380,"height":426},
		{"id":"d9ddefcce0892ca0","type":"text","text":"==Afflictions==","x":-1773,"y":-3008,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"121bbc5851390d17","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Fighting a sin is mentally and physically taxing, and often supernaturally corrupting. Many sin abilities, including those that inflict hooks (see below), inflict ==afflictions==. An affliction is an ongoing, usually negative effect on your exorcist, such as an infection or something more supernatural, such as psychic trauma, corruption, or influence. Afflictions are ==permanent for the duration of the hunt once acquired,== but are ==automatically cleared== after the mission is over. \n> \n> Each sin has a list of unique afflictions it can inflict, or the admin can improvise based on simple parameters.","x":-1841,"y":-3620,"width":386,"height":452},
		{"id":"8fc56a1afbee2242","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> CAIN pays out for the cremation of dead exorcists and allows for a grievance period. Recovering the (even partial) remains of a dead exorcist grants everyone 1 xp and 1 scrip per recovery after the mission is over.","x":-3960,"y":380,"width":386,"height":199},
		{"id":"6ac2a75d4fa9061b","type":"text","text":"==Hooks==","x":-2440,"y":-3424,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"701e20a64d1a5e64","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> During your missions you will be forced to make hard choices or trade offs, or be exposed to situations or harm that will come back to bite you later (sometimes literally). \n> \n> As complications or consequences of your actions, the Admin can choose to give you a ==hook== instead of immediately applying consequences. A hook is a very simple 3 slash talisman that starts empty. You track them on your sheet. \n> \n> You slash a hook under the following situations: \n> \n> • the tension talisman fills out during a hunt \n> \n> • When you roll, and the Admin rolls a ‘1’ on the risk die \n> \n> • If you would gain that hook again","x":-2522,"y":-3222,"width":415,"height":548},
		{"id":"a8117c299644cf2d","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When you create a hook, ==write it as its consequences==, for example: \n> \n> • an exorcist is fighting a sin, and takes a nasty hit. The Admin decides to give them a hook instead of filling up their stress, giving them a ‘festering wound’ hook. \n> \n> • an exorcist decides to steal a key from a hotel owner, but rolls badly on the risk die. Rather than apply consequences right away, the Admin writes ‘wanted by police’ as a hook. \n> \n> • an exorcist decides to push themselves while performing a seance. The Admin writes ‘pass out from exhaustion’ as a hook.","x":-3380,"y":-3140,"width":340,"height":528},
		{"id":"f1230e422129df06","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Whenever ==you would slash a hook a 3rd time and fill it up==, the Admin may ‘==cash it in==’ at any time to erase it and apply its consequences, which are always worse than they would be up front.","x":-2940,"y":-2674,"width":280,"height":268},
		{"id":"1e760a000f8ccdf5","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Additionally: \n> \n> • a character’s own abilities can also give them hooks \n> \n> • hooks ==always inflict more severe consequences== than the alternative (more stress, afflictions, permanent character changes, etc)","x":-3370,"y":-2460,"width":320,"height":303},
		{"id":"1a9f882061f15efd","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> To deal with hooks, abide by the following rules: \n> \n> • ==hooks reset after each mission==, so a character should start a mission with no hooks \n> \n> • when an exorcist ==rests== during a hunt, they can remove slashes on a hook (see pg. 38)","x":-3361,"y":-1980,"width":303,"height":300},
		{"id":"ee5368d060cee7cf","type":"text","text":"==Bargaining==","x":-3559,"y":-1364,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"e48a0e4be4f98343","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Characters are always free to ==suggest hooks== to the Admin as consequences for their own or another’s actions. They are even able to suggest hooks as a payment or tradeoff for the following: \n> \n> • +1D advantage \n> \n> • make an action less hard or risky \n> \n> • use a free psyche burst as part of the action \n> \n> • Slash a talisman an extra time","x":-4056,"y":-1364,"width":296,"height":484},
		{"id":"302165ad647d6336","type":"text","text":"==Kit==","x":-1120,"y":-3500,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"93b52d9c1128c075","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Exorcists are equipped with a standard issue CAIN-issued kit to bring on a mission. You deploy with 5 kit points, which represent your capacity and inventory of items. This is an abstract and you don’t have to decide what you are bringing with you when you embark on a mission - you can pull something out from your listed kit items when it’s needed, as long as you have points left.","x":-1188,"y":-4015,"width":388,"height":315},
		{"id":"6b1c14958871fb8b","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> You can spend the listed number of points to pull out any of the following choices, at any time (each choice gives you all items listed). \n> \n> ==• 0:== Standard issue uniform, collar, shoes, button pin. \n> \n> ==• 1:== Notebook and pen. \n> \n> ==• 1==: Matchbook (20 matches). Clean handkerchief. \n> \n> ==• 2:== Your service weapons: A CAT 0 firearm, ammunition, and melee weapon. This can be upgraded (see pg. 84)","x":-540,"y":-4052,"width":440,"height":390},
		{"id":"7588c394e247423b","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==That’s all you get==. However, you can expand what is available to you in your kit by spending scrip, which you get from completing missions. Scrip must be spent between missions, and you can see a list of items on pg. 78. These items are always available for you. Many of them are useful during a mission. Many of them are not.","x":-513,"y":-3525,"width":387,"height":283},
		{"id":"1346fdca410decf5","type":"text","text":"==Service weapon==","x":-444,"y":-4351,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"36bf515af4014734","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> (Please guide yourselves/me with The Icarus doctrine weaponry addons system for this part )","x":-1040,"y":-4396,"width":360,"height":151},
		{"id":"f780ddad6f05e6d0","type":"text","text":"==Picking up items== ","x":37,"y":-4241,"width":183,"height":50},
		{"id":"bb012db167e21e8b","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> You can pick up anything you can find during a mission and use it with no issues (mark KP as usual). However, you cannot keep it between missions.","x":440,"y":-4299,"width":400,"height":166},
		{"id":"f92b9139cefe836b","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> You can also “contraband” material or equipment found during missions back to base by rolling a 1d6:\n> \n> 1-4: the player is verbally admonished for such a reckless act, consequences will apply to the start of the following operation depending on what was being brought back to base and the severity of the act itself.\n> \n> 5-6: No one notices the player bringing back something hidden on them, the nature and effect if usable must be talk ahead of time with the admin before the next mission, if it is decorative/for flavor it can just be a visual addition and nothing more without problems, items contraband this way can be brought along the following operation without losing kit, but once hunt begins they can’t get acquired until the next one begins.","x":-120,"y":-4888,"width":431,"height":556},
		{"id":"817035f71ee215cb","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> (Addendum: There is now, by personal addition, a contraband-style acquisition mechanic included in the Field Manual!)","x":430,"y":-4690,"width":420,"height":160},
		{"id":"8c6ccd2e869ed7d9","type":"text","text":"==The Hunt==","x":977,"y":1289,"width":250,"height":51},
		{"id":"b98b23489ae95e6e","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> This game is played as a series of ==hunts==. Each hunt may take one or more sessions. Activities pursued during a hunt might require a single roll or might require a talisman to track progress. They might take a single scene or roll or might take a couple scenes - it’s up to the approach the exorcists take and the judgement of the Admin.","x":980,"y":1517,"width":360,"height":323},
		{"id":"16485f2a86e75a07","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Hunts have the following basic format:","x":1041,"y":2060,"width":243,"height":119},
		{"id":"aa039491e7a46f1e","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> 1. Exorcists have a ==briefing== about the upcoming hunt.","x":1011,"y":2300,"width":305,"height":120},
		{"id":"57db7d9cded10049","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> 2. Exorcists ==arrive== on the scene. They always arrive in good shape: ==no stress, no injuries, no hooks, 3 psyche bursts, max kit points==. Essentially, each mission is a ‘reset’ for them. The only thing that carries over for them is agendas, sin level, sin marks, and their own abilities, experience, and scrip.","x":977,"y":2540,"width":372,"height":300},
		{"id":"4fb158cc4b78c231","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> 3. The goal of every hunt is to ==track== down a sin’s refuge - its ==palace== - where it can be permanently destroyed.","x":1014,"y":2960,"width":298,"height":198},
		{"id":"a4621d14b466a53f","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> 4. However, exorcists may want to reach their goal with all the advantages they can get. By investigating the circumstances of the sin’s birth - its ==traumas==, they may be able to weaken it. They may want to ==prepare== to give themselves advantages. Lastly, they may need to ==rest== to recover their strength.","x":996,"y":3280,"width":335,"height":320},
		{"id":"1540f1dfbcda082e","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> 5. While exorcists are investigating and tracking the sin, the situation continues to evolve. This is tracked by the Admin with the ==tension talisman== and a ==pressure talisman.== The tension talisman ticks up as exorcists take action and introduces new complications or consequences. The pressure talisman ticks up whenever the tension talisman fills up and represents the overall degrading situation. If the pressure talisman fills up completely, the situation ==goes out of control== and things get much worse, so the exorcists are on a timer.","x":993,"y":3720,"width":341,"height":509},
		{"id":"3be6ee430f622d8b","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> 6. During a hunt, the players might get into ==conflict== scenes when the stakes get high. If they fight a sin outside its palace, it may always retreat there to recuperate. Once inside a sin’s palace, the exorcists can summon it back there to cause an ==execution== scene where the sin may be permanently destroyed or dealt with.","x":1004,"y":4380,"width":319,"height":380},
		{"id":"3b1348610792d8e9","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> 7. ==Exfiltration==: The exorcists leave the scene. If the exorcists survive, they are paid in scrip and mark a mission survived. They then halve all sin.","x":1021,"y":4880,"width":285,"height":211},
		{"id":"0fbb80851f770fd6","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> At the ==end of every session==, exorcists will get ==experience== based on their activities and agendas.","x":1022,"y":5215,"width":284,"height":165},
		{"id":"609e2e05e2d7ba81","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> (Addendum: In this campaign we will do it ==by every operation/hunt completed== rather than by every end of a session)","x":520,"y":5201,"width":293,"height":194},
		{"id":"cb361da07aa01d5a","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The hunt always starts with a ==briefing==. These nominally should take place in some kind of comfortable or formal location. CAIN has sanctioned office facilities all over the world but exorcists are encouraged to improvise for the sake of expediency and exorcists have been known to take briefings in train cars, private cafe booths, or even fast food restaurants. A briefing scene ==never increases tension== (see pg. 38)","x":1600,"y":2198,"width":400,"height":325},
		{"id":"cb57ddd24b07844f","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The person giving the briefing goes over the broad details of the case, the location, and the points of interest. This person doesn’t necessarily have to be an NPC but could be a player character going over materials received from CAIN. Due to the rapid, secretive, and extremely time sensitive nature of their activities, exorcists are ==faced with multiple problems==:","x":2260,"y":2210,"width":420,"height":301},
		{"id":"0809dd8c035d37fe","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • First, the only thing exorcists know about the sin is its ==basic type== and its ==trauma questions==, (the first page of each sin entry). They also know basic information about the ==inciting incident== that prompted their appearance, such as clues or characters involved. The rest of the Sin sheet, including its powers, history, and abilities, is unrevealed.","x":2960,"y":2217,"width":392,"height":288},
		{"id":"5906bce077baec6b","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • Second, CAIN is a ==clandestine organization. It never starts with the cooperation of local authorities==, who exorcists must work around, suppress, control, or avoid, lest they reveal the true nature of their activities.","x":3540,"y":2246,"width":354,"height":229},
		{"id":"c909bd72eeeab6b5","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • Third, and finally, the exorcists deploy ==only with what is in their kit== .","x":4060,"y":2289,"width":315,"height":144},
		{"id":"330d8a6ef05643ba","type":"text","text":"==Points of interest==","x":4559,"y":2332,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"18c4d4e429857968","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The Admin should lay out all the ==points of interest== for the investigation area. These are locations, details, and people involved in the investigation. The Admin may choose to keep some of these secret but should present at least 2- 3 to the exorcists at the start. These are typically things that kicked off the investigation in the first place - hooks like a murder scene, a sighting of something supernatural, a suspicious individual, or a mysterious disappearance. For more details on this see ==running CAIN== on pg. 94.","x":4980,"y":2186,"width":440,"height":350},
		{"id":"361b6322f52ff3b3","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> After the briefing, the exorcists arrive on the scene. It is traditional for them to arrive in CAIN black helicopters or armored cars but public transit can also make an appearance.","x":1600,"y":2581,"width":319,"height":218},
		{"id":"1f91ce7bd9eb4996","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Exorcists that arrive have a ‘full refresh’ of sorts. They always start with ==no stress, no injuries, no hooks, 3 psyche bursts==, and ==all their kit points==","x":2129,"y":2592,"width":311,"height":196},
		{"id":"9c64872024d190d2","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The only burden an exorcist carries from mission to mission is their current level of sin (after halving at mission end).","x":2640,"y":2603,"width":320,"height":175},
		{"id":"b605e20b4c22656e","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> All sins create a warped pocket reality called a ==palace==, a kind of lair where the world has twisted to accommodate it. Tracking the Sin down to its palace, and tracking down the sin’s host, is one of the main purposes of the exorcists’ mission. A palace is ==always nearby== where the Sin is most active, and is ==always inside the investigation area.==","x":1559,"y":2923,"width":402,"height":273},
		{"id":"484eace32652ac4c","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Exorcists that track a sin might: \n> \n> • use their supernatural abilities to sniff out its traces and taint \n> \n> • try to search for evidence of its palace in the environment \n> \n> • follow clues based on its victims \n> \n> • pull on social connections that its host has \n> \n> • track down its host, and whatever state they are in","x":2129,"y":2901,"width":511,"height":317},
		{"id":"d9b0fee008066ce6","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> A palace is a parasitic extra-dimensional space and its inside may not conform to the rules of mundane reality. Its entrance can be invisible to mundane humans, and is often hidden in plain sight. Its appearance inside often reflects the psyche of the sin and its host","x":2840,"y":2938,"width":380,"height":244},
		{"id":"83eba76858878cb5","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Tracking a sin’s palace down is important, since if fought outside of its palace, a Sin may slip away there when injured to heal and recuperate - it is ==impossible to slay it permanently outside.== If slain outside, the Sin retreats into its palace and regenerates, and is trapped there until pressure increases.","x":3420,"y":2907,"width":340,"height":305},
		{"id":"0c87137bc06b5157","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> If a Sin is fought inside of its palace, however, it can be fully destroyed and has nowhere to retreat to. This forces an ==execution scene.==","x":3960,"y":2963,"width":320,"height":193},
		{"id":"0bc017816d60011f","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Exorcists that enter a sin’s palace can forcibly summon it there if it is outside. All exorcists are able to use the ==Nail of Abel==, a psychic pulse that forces a Sin back into its palace. To use it, however, they need one of the following: \n> \n> • The physical presence of the Sin’s host \n> \n> • An object or person precious to the Sin’s host \n> \n> • A part of the Sin’s hosts’ body, even deceased","x":3955,"y":2474,"width":330,"height":433},
		{"id":"fd719da4eea19e65","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> (Addendum: In this campaign, just finding the palace/\"nest\" is enough to start a execution fight if wandering around it for too long as \"you are intruding, and It is aware of that\")","x":3398,"y":2582,"width":319,"height":218},
		{"id":"85daf7838ecbd357","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Otherwise, brave exorcists can confront and fight a sin outside of its palace and force it there. Exorcists that successfully track down a sin’s palace may not want to rush off to fight it however, as unprepared exorcists are usually dead exorcists.","x":4493,"y":2942,"width":382,"height":236},
		{"id":"22d807058b5412c5","type":"text","text":"==Investigation (A)==","x":1548,"y":3410,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b8b0c9664c5f4b0b","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Aside from tracking the sin, exorcists may want to take time to gather information. The main purpose of this is to investigate the sin’s capabilities or its traumas. \n> \n> Investigating may take only a few rolls to make progress, but depending on the exorcist’s approach, the goal of the exorcist’s activities, and the complexity of gathering information, the Admin can set out a talisman to roll on.","x":1964,"y":3251,"width":330,"height":378},
		{"id":"9c2e6e62023ccdd5","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Exorcists that investigate might \n> \n> • examine the sin’s victims \n> \n> • observe or attempt to communicate with the sin \n> \n> • examine crime scenes or traces of the sin \n> \n> • gather information from witnesses or contacts \n> \n> • perform occult rituals or research","x":2493,"y":3228,"width":294,"height":424},
		{"id":"114fcda139811b8a","type":"text","text":" ==Traumas==","x":2928,"y":3408,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"42a0869b41e85dbf","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> All Sins have traumas, events that have precipitated their birth from their host. When making a Sin, the Admin answers the sin’s trauma questions and establishes traumas based on the answers. In essence, traumas are at the center of the human tragedy that forms the core of hunts. \n> \n> Exorcists start a hunt knowing the sin’s type, and therefore its trauma questions (but not the answers).","x":3360,"y":3269,"width":400,"height":339},
		{"id":"9f7e486c07438a41","type":"text","text":"==Countering== ","x":3900,"y":3409,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"8d681dc175ae7917","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> If they are able to investigate and uncover traumas by discovering their answers, when exorcists fight the sin in an execution scene and the sin reacts to their actions, they can use each answer to ==counter== a sin’s reaction. \n> \n> Exorcists must use a counter ==after== seeing the result of the risk die but ==before== the GM announces a reaction.","x":4315,"y":3276,"width":357,"height":325},
		{"id":"651abf25e0702189","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Then, roll 1d3. ==Reduce any stress sufffered by one target of its reaction by the amount on the die==, and the sin immediately takes ==that many slashes== on its execution talisman from the psychic trauma, which cannot reduce it below 1.","x":4875,"y":3317,"width":360,"height":244},
		{"id":"de5300f27ad3b1eb","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Exorcists that counter a sin’s reaction may: \n> \n> • Make appeals to its humanity \n> \n> • Throw it off guard with sensitive information \n> \n> • Enrage or distract it \n> \n> • Reveal a weakness Each question can ==only be used once==!","x":5440,"y":3273,"width":360,"height":333},
		{"id":"4619b19d8010d135","type":"text","text":"==Preparation (B)==","x":6000,"y":3410,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"888e1cb489e2beba","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Exorcists may want to make preparations to confront the sin or constructing defenses against its domains. \n> \n> Exorcists can either prepare countermeasures against sin’s powers, work on assets to help them fight or track down the sin, or they can work on any other projects that will take time to prepare.","x":6420,"y":3268,"width":340,"height":344},
		{"id":"f4e568dc55528449","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> For example: \n> \n> • writing talismans to counteract against a Sin’s power \n> \n> • preparing a trap \n> \n> • crafting improvised explosives \n> \n> • trying to convince civilians to leave the area \n> \n> • drawing a protective circle around a character \n> \n> • locating a working car","x":6940,"y":3232,"width":360,"height":416},
		{"id":"6318ef69160e8dbc","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> In some cases, preparing may only take a single action roll, but for more lengthy or ambitious projects, the Admin should set out a talisman to roll on.","x":7460,"y":3338,"width":341,"height":205},
		{"id":"5f1d88c52486b8b2","type":"text","text":"==Rest (C)==","x":8020,"y":3410,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"868afec464ce4f63","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Exorcists that rest are recovering and letting time pass. \n> \n> The exorcists must decide to rest as a group. If they do, pressure always increases by 1","x":8460,"y":3324,"width":320,"height":234},
		{"id":"79c32a4e2c769e88","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Instead of spending a psyche burst, you can gain ==1d3 sin==. This includes any use of psyche burst, such as spending psyche burst for +1D on rolls or using a psyche blast. If you’d spend multiple psyche bursts, roll all these d3s at the same time.","x":333,"y":-1401,"width":361,"height":274},
		{"id":"b0c0ef4aba6e6f91","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Each exorcist ==rolls 2d3==, then takes each die and assigns it to an item from the following list. They are able to make the same choice twice. \n> \n> • Regain that many psyche bursts \n> \n> • Erase stress equal to the d3 \n> \n> • Erase slashes on a hook equal to the d3 Resting also ends the effects of some powers and abilities, or resets others.","x":8970,"y":3286,"width":430,"height":309},
		{"id":"614b00c7eec45fa4","type":"text","text":"==Tension (A)==","x":1616,"y":3942,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"bae52cb500f86226","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> At the start of the hunt, the Admin will set out a ==tension talisman==, visible to everyone. Tension increases when: \n> \n> ==• The scene passes== \n> \n> ==• A 1 result is rolled on a risk die==, but no more than once a scene","x":2001,"y":3847,"width":359,"height":250},
		{"id":"4cc6d91b8c49a0da","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Not all exorcist activities can take a scene - some can be rolled in the background of other scenes, like preparation activities. If a scene is especially short, or two scenes are happening at the same time, the Admin might not count it. \n> \n> However, the general rule is whenever a scene passes, tension is ticked up. ==Each time the tension talisman goes to 3 slashes, empty it==. Then the Admin increases the ==pressure talisman== (see below) by 1 and picks a ==tension move== to activate.","x":2611,"y":3778,"width":409,"height":388},
		{"id":"12318a4989f7da54","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Tension moves often improve a Sin’s position, impede or harm the exorcists, or have some other deleterious effect on the world (see pg 104 for a list of them). \n> \n> Tension never increases during a conflict scene (see pg 42) or a briefing scene (see pg 36).","x":3196,"y":3831,"width":329,"height":282},
		{"id":"3e63cd694d864754","type":"text","text":"==Pressure (B)==","x":3740,"y":3942,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"ddae6a4208cb59eb","type":"text","text":"![[Pasted image 20261018194046.png]]","x":3740,"y":3801,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"69f52d05cec4ba9c","type":"text","text":"![[Pasted image 20261018192327.png]]","x":1616,"y":3787,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"35ad7796abedc2bd","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Each sin has a unique way that it affects or warps the world around it. This effect gets ==worse and worse== the longer time goes on. \n> \n> At the start of a hunt, the Admin will set out a ==pressure talisman==, visible to everyone, with a capacity of 6. As the talisman fills up, ==the situation degrades==, in a way unique to each sin.","x":4200,"y":3819,"width":380,"height":307},
		{"id":"91611139c9ab69fe","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Pressure increases by 1 when exorcists ==rest==, and ==when the tension talisman fills up==. \n> \n> The sin’s ==execution talisman increases by 1 for each tick on pressure==. Additionally, if the pressure talisman ==fills up completely== (to 6) the situation gets ==out of control==. The effects of this are noted in each sin’s pressure description.","x":4820,"y":3810,"width":360,"height":326},
		{"id":"4903ab2b5a68437a","type":"text","text":"==Execution==","x":1540,"y":4540,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"416e5e0c8bf076d1","type":"text","text":"==Call for extraction (D)==","x":9540,"y":3411,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"11e083e9ff9f5018","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ...once the team encounters the palace of the sin they can report the area alongside any information acquired along the way back to their handler/division representative and start an “execution scene” with the sin in question adding a 4 slashes long talisman called \"reinforcements” that gets one slash per round passed or time, once completed with the Assault team arrival, the group of players can leave the place and get extracted away of it to a specific safehouse on the city to await debriefing about the results of the operation...","x":9940,"y":3295,"width":561,"height":292},
		{"id":"58351aa6795d728a","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Each sin has an ==execution talisman==. Broadly speaking, the execution talisman determines how the Sin functions in a ==conflict scene==. It also gives the exorcists a talisman to roll against when going up against the Sin in a conflict situation. It’s the lifeblood of the sin - when exorcists attack or otherwise attempt to take action to ==harm== or ==defeat== the Sin, it’s this talisman that fills up. ==The talisman is equal to 8 + pressure + its category==","x":1960,"y":4426,"width":460,"height":288},
		{"id":"38314c1de2375c32","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The following rules apply when fighting a Sin: \n> \n> • All rolls in a conflict scene ==always start risky== by default \n> \n> • Sins ==react== differently to the exorcist’s actions ==based on the risk roll==. They may lash out, create a threat or obstacle, or initiate a dangerous attack. There is a list of reactions for the Admin to improvise on each sin sheet \n> \n> • Exorcists can invoke one of the sin’s traumas to counter a reaction, reducing the stress it inflicts and directly dealing damage to a sin \n> \n> • If fought outside its palace, a Sin is shunted back to its palace when it takes ==4 slashes== or it’s ==reduced to 0==, and cannot leave until pressure increases. It heals either when confronted, or when pressure increases, erasing ==1d3 slashes== on its talisman, or ==2d3== if it has 0 slashes remaining \n> \n> • If a sin is fought inside its palace, it ==cannot flee==, and must fight in an execution scene until its defeat.","x":2740,"y":4335,"width":600,"height":470},
		{"id":"eedbdfa1bec5b3fb","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When the talisman is filled up completely in an execution scene, the Sin is ==at the mercy== of the exorcists. They have a choice: to ==Execute== or ==Fail==.","x":3540,"y":4489,"width":354,"height":163},
		{"id":"e7cafb6eb507ff18","type":"text","text":"==Execute==","x":4040,"y":4541,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"2644cac8f8f6facb","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> (Addendum: In this campaign this is only reachable by calling extraction and killing it in the 4 rounds window of time, and it is always for the host and worm, no mercy for the Exiled)","x":4002,"y":4220,"width":326,"height":224},
		{"id":"71dbf7ed08a86947","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The execution is complete; divine judgement has been passed, the world was returned to an inert state. \n> \n> ==Executing== a sin makes the mission a success and pays out ==5 scrip== to each exorcist. Executing a sin is usually not immediately fatal for a living host, but is extremely traumatic, and the host is nearly always taken under custody of CAIN for monitoring, rehabilitation, or possible recruitment.","x":4520,"y":4406,"width":420,"height":330},
		{"id":"75849a64e4a250e9","type":"text","text":"==Fail==","x":5080,"y":4541,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b7d8a7db32d1d069","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The execution was a failure; the stain was not wiped away. \n> \n> ==Failing== an execution by perishing as a group, fleeing the investigation, or refusing to execute a vulnerable sin, immediately ends the hunt. CAIN will call in ==total expurgation== of the area via orbital rail drop to prevent a sin event, and advise the exorcists to exfiltrate (if they are alive). Surviving exorcists will be appropriately disciplined and also ==docked 1 scrip==. Necessary coverup, casualties, and cleanup will be extensive.","x":5540,"y":4402,"width":480,"height":339},
		{"id":"fee2a73feebce96c","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> (Addendum: Allowing the worm to reach Stage 2 will be considered a failure on the side of the team if its palace is not reported back to the Assault Division before reaching this point)","x":5057,"y":4220,"width":297,"height":244},
		{"id":"fc2ffbb25d5bb3d6","type":"text","text":"==Unless.== \n\nIf at ==least one trauma question== has been answered and unveiled to the Sin…","x":6260,"y":4483,"width":312,"height":178},
		{"id":"989c7b0b51112a40","type":"text","text":"==Spare==","x":6700,"y":4542,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"38fd48f8374b12bf","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> it is rumored (in hushed whispers) a Sin may be ==spared== and brought back from the brink, crushed under the weight of its own contradictions, and forced to recede back into the psyche.\n> \n> When this happens, the host may (bit by bit, part by part) regain control.","x":7140,"y":4443,"width":360,"height":258},
		{"id":"bf6ff2cc660e3fe8","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> In such a case, the execution is still deemed a failure, but since there is no Sin detected by Cain in the area, there is no reason to order expurgation. The exorcists are recalled and ==paid 3 scrip for hazard pay==. They are then debriefed, questioned, then sent to their next assignment. \n> \n> Sparing a sin is a judgement call. Can you afford to risk the spread of the stain? Is the mercy in your heart a weakness?","x":7680,"y":4402,"width":382,"height":340},
		{"id":"e8d30b0c94472adc","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> From Administration:\n> \n> The Exiled are stained beyond salvation, even though their final crime may have been the final straw of a hundred, or a desperate act.\n> \n> You will still be allowed 3 Warehouse permits for a operation accomplished.\n> \n> Do not repeat this act, The Exiled can be justified in present, but in past or future, Do not question your own or The Corporation goals beyond this point.","x":7140,"y":3980,"width":360,"height":422},
		{"id":"444804ac5ddaf2c5","type":"text","text":"==Not reporting the Palace/\"Nest\"==","x":8235,"y":4541,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"c521b21ef64bfee3","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Although there is another option if the team agrees to go along with it:  Do not report the palace back and instead of following standard protocol you can all try together to ==finish the job==.\n> \n>   \n> \n> For this being not something expected by The Administration, as there is not failsafes in place if something goes wrong until is too late to be noticed, the group personnel elimination of the worm without any reinforcements or properly prepared operatives implies that there is no one to depend if something goes wrong, as the situation quickly develops to a fight to the death for both groups: ==The worm kills you or you kill the worm.==\n> \n> ","x":8660,"y":4414,"width":631,"height":315},
		{"id":"5121f41f6af28acf","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Once the team reports back after surviving their “reckless act” to a designated safehouse for not only debriefing about the successful operation but to be verbally admonished by Administration personnel for the decision taken during the operation, although the team is expected to still receive an execution payment as is due (5 script) ==and== a bonus given under the table to each operative hours or days later after the fact (Bonus 3 script) for generally an anonymous source still trying to incentive such activity without being openly public about it for safety concerns.\n> \n> This decision generally must be taken groupally in and out of character for the sake of having everyone in the idea rather than taking a reckless action when no one is in the state of doing such a thing, as this is essentially starting a fight to the death without any failsafes in place for anyone involved.\n\n","x":9500,"y":4371,"width":618,"height":402},
		{"id":"94287ddae309c0d0","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Hunters are exfiltrated from the area, usually in CAIN black helicopters, then brought back to the nearest CAIN post or carrier vessel for debriefing.","x":1482,"y":4902,"width":367,"height":168},
		{"id":"9e810c7a98d1e008","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • If the execution was successful they are each paid ==5 scrip==. If the execution was a failure they are docked ==1 scrip==. If the sin was spared, they gain ==3 scrip== \n> \n> • They gain ==1 xp== and ==1 scrip== for each dead exorcist they recover. \n> \n> • They ==halve all sin==. \n> \n> • They can ==mark a mission survived== and potentially rank up in category\n> \n> They are then allowed a period of rest before they proceed to their next mission.","x":2080,"y":4804,"width":540,"height":365},
		{"id":"c490b87b8c1e1f02","type":"text","text":"==Conflict scenes==","x":560,"y":4540,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"c763000c98eb8b33","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The rules so far have been about freeform play. If the group feels like a situation calls for even more structured play, they can use the conflict rules. These rules are for playing out scenes that are exactly as they sound - a ==conflict scene==, usually with two or more sides, such as a battle. \n> \n> Conflicts are usually violent, but don’t necessarily have to rely on violence - depending on context they could also be used to play out a chase, a stealth sequence, a friendly contest, or a trial.","x":-40,"y":4396,"width":422,"height":348},
		{"id":"c28dbf95c06c80d5","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Conflict scenes have slightly different rules to regular, freeform play: \n> \n> • A conflict scene can be started when the ==stakes of the scene are high== and there are ==two or more sides in opposition.== \n> \n> • All actions taken in conflict scenes ==always start risky== by default. Otherwise, they wouldn’t be conflict scenes!\n> \n> • ==Tension never increases== during a conflict scene, and tension doesn’t increase due to a conflict scene passing.","x":-620,"y":4371,"width":380,"height":399},
		{"id":"9d36550ef079b3d2","type":"text","text":"==1: Establish stakes==","x":-1196,"y":4538,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"128689c6518e4e7d","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> At the start of a conflict scene, both sides establish what they want out of the conflict. Sometime this is very simple (I want to escape, I want you to listen to me, I want you dead). These are the ==stakes== of the scene. If the opposite side is willing to budge on the stakes, then there probably isn't a conflict - or find out new stakes. You can use them as a good guide to how each side should act, and also as to when the scene should end or one side should give up.","x":-1740,"y":4390,"width":420,"height":357},
		{"id":"a6bad9928e7dcb90","type":"text","text":"==2: Establish the scene and set out talismans.==","x":-2195,"y":4536,"width":250,"height":62},
		{"id":"90382f465a761ca7","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Where are the characters? Where are their opponents? What is the location and what resources are available? \n> \n> Also set out any ==talismans== you expect to use during the scene.","x":-2640,"y":4437,"width":300,"height":261},
		{"id":"892b4bb0f611f493","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> These talismans should represent important tasks, goals, or parameters for the scene. For a fight with a Sin, you usually use the ==Execution talisman==, for example. If you’re just trying to escape it, however, you might use an ‘escape’ talisman representing your ability to get away. You might set out talismans representing obstacles, opponents, or time limits.","x":-2640,"y":4840,"width":300,"height":380},
		{"id":"b21d2fd12556d215","type":"text","text":"==3: Players choose actions==","x":-3100,"y":4536,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"ce8b6941bbf442d6","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The player characters (the exorcists) ==always act first in a conflict scene==. When a player acts, they can choose one of the following courses of action. The actions are descriptive, not proscriptive. Characters should generally think about what they want to do in a scene, then try and think about what action their activities fall under.","x":-3600,"y":4395,"width":320,"height":346},
		{"id":"0c916e4e6dd7797d","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==• Act:== Take direct action against one of the goals, talismans, or obstacles in a fight. In very simple terms, this could just be fighting your opponent. It could also be driving a car, launching a verbal assault, neutralizing poison, healing a dying person, or sneaking in through a vent.","x":-3600,"y":4880,"width":320,"height":300},
		{"id":"cd38f2408339e96e","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==• Defend:== Prepare to defend an ally. Choose an ally. The next time that ally takes consequences while you are defending them, roll 1d6. On a 1-3, you reduce stress suffered by them by 1, but also take 1 stress. On a 4/5 you don’t take stress, and on a 6 reduce stress suffered by your target by 2 instead. This effect does not stack from multiple defenders.","x":-3600,"y":5300,"width":320,"height":360},
		{"id":"9a1a14d6c54834b4","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==• Set Up or Teamwork:== Perform teamwork with an ally in another action, or take a set up action. These are identical to the activities described on pg. 15-16.","x":-3600,"y":5820,"width":320,"height":200},
		{"id":"dc47f551d65509d2","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==• Analyze:== Gather information during the scene. This can progress a talisman, create a new talisman, or reveal the capabilities of opponents. Analyze can be used to change the parameters of the scene or create an opening or opportunity, like a new route in a chase, or a gap in an opponent’s fighting style. This is a more permanent change than a set up action and is typically more powerful. Talismans created by Analyze can grant advantage dice, make a situation less hard, or make it less risky","x":-3600,"y":6180,"width":320,"height":460},
		{"id":"a09d0a0b00f70a7a","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==• Flee==: Get out of the conflict scene. The Admin might require a talisman for this if it’s hard to leave. Once out, a character is ==out for the rest of the scene== but is ==considered safe==.","x":-3600,"y":6787,"width":320,"height":213},
		{"id":"600e7570b8294321","type":"text","text":"==4: Characters act== ","x":-4020,"y":4536,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"a552e915cf8df031","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Characters can act one at a time, in any order. Characters that are performing teamwork or defending another character don’t trigger a risk roll from an opponent. ==Any other action does==, including those that don’t normally make that roll, unless someone has specifically set up that action to be less risky. When all player characters have taken their action, ==a round passes==. \n> \n> Some effects from abilities rely on rounds and the Admin might set a round time limit in order to keep things concise.","x":-4500,"y":4342,"width":351,"height":448},
		{"id":"07a5f9a8307b486d","type":"text","text":"==5: Opponents react==","x":-4900,"y":4536,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"64696d498db48a91","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Instead of taking their own actions, NPCs or obstacles the Admin controls in the scene will use the ==risk die to inform their reactions==. Each time a player takes an action, such as attack, analyze, or set up, the Admin uses the risk die and reacts in any way they see fit, targeting any opponent they choose. If the action wasn’t risky, they don’t react. These reactions can often be found as a list of suggestions, such as on a sin’s sheet. The Admin can only take ==one reaction== at once if they have multiple to choose from or multiple opponents are present, with some exceptions. \n> \n> These reactions often ==inflict stress== on the exorcists, change the stakes of the situation with a ==complication==, or ==threaten== them with worse consequences. The Admin is always free to not use a proscribed reaction, but instead to improvise an outcome as normal based on the die result, fiction, and circumstances.","x":-5540,"y":4288,"width":440,"height":557},
		{"id":"e5b8a5266f64f7c6","type":"text","text":"==Concluding a conflict scene==","x":-6040,"y":4537,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"10cfc47814fd7fc5","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Play continues until one side has accomplished their goals or settled the stakes, or other parameters such as a time limit have been reached.","x":-6480,"y":4470,"width":320,"height":195},
		{"id":"726f9bca58293abf","type":"text","text":"==Ways to increase in power as a exorcist==","x":-674,"y":-4678,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b67a42b8d4537b51","type":"text","text":"==Experience==","x":-674,"y":-4918,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"21d4e4edbe667c84","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • Did you ==survive?== \n> \n> • Did you ==follow your first agenda item?== \n> \n> • Did you ==follow at least one bolded agenda item?== \n> \n> • Did you take an ==injury== or ==affliction?==","x":-1180,"y":-5318,"width":322,"height":332},
		{"id":"d69690f2beff0ca2","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> You earn experience at the ==end of a session== by answering the checklist questions on your sheet. At the end of a session, exorcists gain experience. Go down the checklist and for every ‘yes’ answer, ==gain 1 xp==.","x":-696,"y":-5282,"width":295,"height":260},
		{"id":"3f7dc0758bad982e","type":"text","text":"==(Addendum: ----)==","x":-246,"y":-5182,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"a3027d90a7cd4248","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When an exorcists’s experience bar is full (4 segments) they can empty it and cash it out for an ==advance==. \n> \n> Some hunts may take more or less sessions - meaning exorcists may have more or less opportunities to earn experience during each hunt.","x":-710,"y":-5740,"width":324,"height":300},
		{"id":"fe0aa8fb3daa34a6","type":"text","text":"==Advances==","x":-220,"y":-5828,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"0f9366b7e4cb3f85","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Any time your experience bar is full (4 segments), you cash it out for an ==advance==. You can bank these advances and spend an advance after a session in the following ways:","x":220,"y":-5894,"width":340,"height":192},
		{"id":"04eed13fc22e4f95","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • Gain a ==new agenda ability== \n> \n> • Gain a ==new blasphemy power== or unlock a ==new blasphemy== and gain its passive. \n> \n> • ==Evolve a sin mark== and roll for a new ability for that sin mark. On duplicate, choose. \n> \n> • Gain ==3 scrip== \n> \n> • ==Improve a skill by 1==. You can do this six times, total, and have ==only two skills at 3 maximum.==","x":220,"y":-5571,"width":340,"height":419},
		{"id":"5e092d12be7df443","type":"text","text":"==Survival==","x":-1152,"y":-4678,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"491bfe5c758da422","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> If you ==survive== enough missions, you qualify for an ==advance in category rating==. You only needed to have participated in a mission and survived - the experience and training from fieldwork is usually enough to hone your powers. Each category rating requires a number of total missions survived.","x":-1640,"y":-4781,"width":380,"height":267},
		{"id":"36993cde12651c6b","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==• Category 2==: 1 mission survived. \n> \n> ==• Category 3==: 2 total missions survived. \n> \n> ==• Category 4==: 4 total missions survived. \n> \n> ==• Category 5==: 7 total missions survived. \n> \n> Increasing in category ups the general capabilities of your powers.","x":-2220,"y":-4795,"width":400,"height":281},
		{"id":"9634a6735c30ed8a","type":"text","text":"(Addendum: for the sake of campaign pacing, we are temporarily, as of Chapter 1, pushing up the CAT increase by \"x2\" its base values, making CAT 2 needing 2 missions rather 1 and CAT 3 needing 4 and so on, assume things will go back to normal once Chapter 2 is reached properly :3","x":-2203,"y":-5140,"width":367,"height":213},
		{"id":"697d4b9db25438a6","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Agendas broadly describe your character’s ==approach to problems== rather than being descriptive of your character as a whole.","x":-1736,"y":-2094,"width":371,"height":168},
		{"id":"89c944cce9bc0b9f","type":"text","text":"==Tags==","x":621,"y":-1734,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"eb57b395edd57739","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==Duration tags== describe how long a power lasts before its effects expire. \n> \n> ==• Instant==: Lasts only a few moments, then expires\n> \n> ==• Until rest==: Lasts until rest \n> \n> ==• 1 scene==: Lasts until the scene passes. \n> \n> ==• Entire Hunt==: Lasts all hunt","x":597,"y":-2242,"width":299,"height":360},
		{"id":"1a8d45432e3bfb41","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==Effect type tags== describe advanced effects or powers you can create with a blasphemy. Effects with the same tag ==do not stack== with each other - the most recently used power replaces the last one. Not all powers have effect tags.","x":437,"y":-2577,"width":320,"height":260},
		{"id":"009601e6d02d3a0e","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==• Curse==: A power affecting another being, usually negatively \n> \n> ==• Charm==: A power affecting yourself or another being, usually positively \n> \n> ==• Transmute==: An effect that affects or alters the physical world in some way \n> \n> ==• Summon==: An effect that creates a psychic construction or entity","x":-3,"y":-2407,"width":409,"height":331},
		{"id":"e03305505bdcbd7c","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> A player can decide which skill to use when taking an action. ==However, the Admin is free to tell them if using that skill would be risky or hard, or not effective== given the circumstances. \n> \n> You can apply only one skill at a time to an action, but you probably have the choice of multiple skills for the same situation.","x":-216,"y":-2836,"width":321,"height":348},
		{"id":"1c034858c26018b9","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Roll 1d6 for mark, then 1d6 on that mark for ability. If you gain a mark from a source other than sin overflow, you must still randomly roll for the ability.","x":1700,"y":-3967,"width":406,"height":180},
		{"id":"ef6c58b8086985ae","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • If you have a mark already and roll it again, the mark evolves, granting it a new ability (re-roll duplicates). \n> \n> • If you roll a ‘6’ for a mark, you can choose which one, then roll for the ability as normal \n> \n> • You increase your resistance rolls by 1 for each sin mark you have. \n> \n> • Sin marks can affect whether rolls are hard or risky, and some grant bonus die or special abilities","x":1675,"y":-4440,"width":457,"height":360},
		{"id":"5c92c25264d1b753","type":"text","text":"==1: Eyes==","x":2280,"y":-3907,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b5586fc7dd2960e4","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Appearance or Manifestation: Black or altered sclera, white iris, split, slit, or duplicate pupil. \n> \n> 1. You can see clearly up to extreme distance, as if you could ‘zoom in’ your vision \n>    \n> 2. You can see through walls and nonliving matter in short distance \n>    \n> 3. When closed, you can sense the ambient emotional state of nearby humans or exorcists. Once a hunt, gain +1D when acting on this. \n>    \n> 4. Once a hunt, you can momentarily paralyze a human by merely looking at them. The effect lasts until you break eye contact, a minute passes, or either of you suffer harm. \n>    \n> 5. You can see clearly in the dark and are unaffected by darkness, weather, or obscurement. \n>    \n> 6. Gain +1D to Surveillance or Investigation. This could put you up to 4D base.","x":2215,"y":-3730,"width":381,"height":670},
		{"id":"69e75882613a8a71","type":"text","text":"==2: Jaw==","x":2760,"y":-3907,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"993e2eacb3dfa0ca","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Appearance or Manifestation: Split or extended jaw, pharyngeal jaw, fangs, black tongue, viscous saliva. \n> \n> 1. You can spit black venom up to short distance. It’s a mundane ranged weapon with about the same effectiveness as a pistol. \n>    \n> 2. You can gain 1d3 sin to re-roll any roll requiring speech, taking the second result as final. \n>    \n> 3. You can whisper short messages into the wind of 6 words or less that a target of your choice can hear in their ear within long distance. The target cannot reply. \n>    \n> 4. You gain +1D when negotiating with, commanding, or convincing humans \n>    \n> 5. Once a hunt, you can give a short, one word command to a human, who then immediately attempts to follow it to the best of their ability. Humans will not obey obviously harmful commands. \n>    \n> 6. Gain +1D to Authority, Connection, or Negotiation. This could put you up to 4D base.","x":2694,"y":-3733,"width":386,"height":733},
		{"id":"935c4242c10d559c","type":"text","text":"==3: Back or chest==","x":3240,"y":-3907,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b96b73a16a4aa4ed","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Appearance or Manifestation: Spines, lesions, skin discoloration or warping, rapid regeneration, hardened or loose skin or scales, vestigial wings, extra ribs. \n> \n> 1. You can roll an extra resting die while resting. If you do, gain the same amount of sin. \n>    \n> 2. You no longer need to breathe. You are no longer affected by mundane toxin or poison. You cannot become intoxicated by alcohol. \n>    \n> 3. When you rest, you can gain 1d3 sin to remove an affliction or hook. \n>    \n> 4. You have a chance of ignoring any injury (roll a d6, ignore on a 6) \n>    \n> 5. Automatically erase 1 stress when pressure increases. \n>    \n> 6. Gain +1D to Conditioning. This could put you up to 4D base.","x":3125,"y":-3733,"width":480,"height":492},
		{"id":"58d4ac5e50810ec8","type":"text","text":"==4: Arms or hands==","x":3720,"y":-3907,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"c836395c4ee4b861","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Appearance or Manifestation: Claws, split hand or harm, extra arm, twisting skin or muscles, discoloration, extra fingers. \n> \n> 1. You can gain 1d3 sin when performing a feat of physical strength to increase the CAT of that feat by +2 \n>    \n> 2. Once a hunt, you may merge any single mundane weapon or item you could hold into your flesh, able to conceal or produce it at will. \n>    \n> 3. Once a hunt, you can dissolve all non-living matter in a cube about ½ CAT size into a black sludge with a touch of your third (ring) finger. \n>    \n> 4. Once a hunt, you can gain 1d3 sin to morph your arm or hand into a melee weapon for a scene. It is a supernatural melee weapon of ½ CAT potency. \n>    \n> 5. You can re-roll any force or interfacing roll, taking the second result as final. If you do, gain 1d3 sin. \n>    \n> 6. Gain +1D to Force or Coordination. This could put you up to 4D base.","x":3625,"y":-3733,"width":440,"height":613},
		{"id":"f9a9c8c991f59e35","type":"text","text":"==5: Skin, hair or legs==","x":4200,"y":-3907,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"177014a04f5d7f3d","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Appearance or Manifestation: Major discoloration or bleaching, transparent skin, warping of gait, digitigrade or multiple legs, patchiness, change in hair color. \n> \n> 1. You can gain 1d3 sin to leap up to an object CAT size in height without rolling. You can do this as part of an action roll. \n>    \n> 2. While touching them with your bare flesh, you can can climb or walk on walls as though they were flat surfaces \n>    \n> 3. You have +1D when running or sprinting on open ground, such as a road \n>    \n> 4. You can gain 1 sin during an action roll to glide a long distance through the air as part of the action. You have to start at height to gain this benefit. \n>    \n> 5. You can gain 1d3 sin to go completely invisible to mundane perception for the duration of an action roll. \n>    \n> 6. Gain +1D to Covert or Interfacing. This could put you up to 4D base","x":4116,"y":-3733,"width":419,"height":609},
		{"id":"abaac30e10cff3d3","type":"text","text":"==6: Choose== ","x":4660,"y":-3907,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"4336e0109e0ec764","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==Mundane Weaponry== \n> \n> Fighting a sin with mundane abilities or tools, such as human capabilities or the swords and sidearms of exorcists, ==is always hard==. It may even be completely ineffective if the sin is many categories higher. To have a good shot at harming a sin, exorcists will need to use their blasphemies, upgrade their service weapons, or get creative.","x":2797,"y":4960,"width":486,"height":289},
		{"id":"2df5a2c599cae1d1","type":"text","text":"==Tension moves==","x":5655,"y":3980,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"9ac635aa996d8061","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When the talisman fills, the Admin can empty the talisman, then pick one of the following ==tension moves== to activate, or improvise based on the ones provided. Tension moves add complications or twists to the narrative and keep the exorcists on their toes. Domains will often add new tension moves to the list or provide hints as to what kind of actions the Admin should take. ==Don’t regard these lists as proscriptive== - rather as a guideline or spread of easily available choices. The Admin is free to to any of these things based on the narrative. Tension moves just give them a golden opportunity to do so. ==The basic moves are:==","x":6620,"y":3720,"width":445,"height":400},
		{"id":"96a6d593678af3dc","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==1: Send Minions.==\n> \n> Self explanatory - send minions after the exorcists. Sins often have traces - lesser sins, or humans that follow their command, listed in each sin description. The Admin can use this tension move to have them show up in a scene unexpectedly - even when the exorcists are otherwise preoccupied!","x":7631,"y":3748,"width":349,"height":345},
		{"id":"8a1fce5d9e37d2a4","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==2: Ambush the exorcists.==\n> \n> The sin attacks the exorcists directly, often with the element of surprise. If fought this way, it is banished back to its palace if it takes 4 slashes on its talisman or is reduced to 0. It must remain there until pressure increases.","x":8145,"y":3795,"width":396,"height":252},
		{"id":"99c18ab0062e2c49","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==3: Involve authorities== \n> \n> The sin attacks the exorcists directly, often with the element of surprise. If fought this way, it is banished back to its palace if it takes 4 slashes on its talisman or is reduced to 0. It must remain there until pressure increases.","x":8700,"y":3795,"width":404,"height":252},
		{"id":"8f543b1954edc496","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==4: Separate someone.==\n> \n> Send an exorcist or important NPC somewhere dangerous or separated - like the Sin’s palace, enemy territory, have them fall through a floor in a ruined building - force the exorcists to split up or sidetrack.","x":9260,"y":3795,"width":364,"height":252},
		{"id":"0312f68b2af15f43","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==5: Force a difficult choice.==\n> \n> Make the exorcists choose between two things they want or need, and take the other thing away once they have made their choice. For example, kill all the civilians trapped in the subway if they choose to chase after the sin’s host now, or let the sin’s host get away if they choose to save the civilians.","x":9760,"y":3785,"width":430,"height":273},
		{"id":"3cac8e15d487b8b2","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==6: Escalate the situation.==\n> \n> Very simply, punch up the tension of the situation. If it’s a calm conversation with local authorities, it becomes a potential arrest. If it’s a simple car ride, the exorcists are suddenly being tailed (by who?). Show the exorcists they are not alone in the abandoned building. Start asking hard questions. Light a fire.","x":10340,"y":3777,"width":410,"height":289},
		{"id":"396e16b02d42209f","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==7: Afflict the exorcists.==\n> \n> Give an exorcist a random afflliction from the sin sheet, or give them a hook with bad consequences attached such as harm, corruption, attention, or attrition.","x":10920,"y":3816,"width":426,"height":211},
		{"id":"613a334fdece49b0","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==8: Start or progress a ticking clock.==\n> \n> Write a talisman for what it represents: air left in the room, time until the poison takes hold, number of innocents left alive, time until the bomb goes off. Write a talisman with 2/3 segments for short, 4+ for long, the shorter the harder. Then fill it up, slashing it ether each time pressure increases (for a bigger situation) or each time a scene passes (for a smaller situation) or each time an action is taken by the exorcists (for an immediate situation). It happens if the talisman is full and would be slashed again. Exorcists should be able to take action to ameliorate the situation, perhaps by filling up a competing talisman, or removing slashes on the current one.","x":11520,"y":3730,"width":520,"height":384},
		{"id":"69382016101c2e64","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==9: Use a domain.==\n> \n> Many sins have domains that give them specific tension moves, and can be easily referred to.","x":11628,"y":4371,"width":412,"height":187},
		{"id":"c585245c6a1f937e","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==10: Threaten or twist an NPC.==\n> \n> Put crosshairs on an NPC, and pull the trigger if the exorcists don’t react. Sins are supernatural forces with tremendous reach, and exorcists strange, ill-socialized, and secretive investigators that are easy to dislike. Threaten to kill, steal, or warp an NPC. Change their attitude or show the effect of some corruption or supernatural force on them. Threaten to have them consumed by the forces beyond their control.\n","x":11000,"y":4300,"width":451,"height":330},
		{"id":"45adf8cfc1b63487","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==11: Introduce a new obstacle.==\n> \n> Put something in the path of the exorcists - an unexpected police presence, a security system, a locked door, a broken bridge, a bevy of teeth gnashing monsters. Then let them figure it out.","x":10400,"y":4348,"width":377,"height":234}
	],
